fix(a11y): the quiet ladder is not a text colour either, and the button holds still

Two corrections to the commit before this one, both found by measuring rather
than by reading.

The sizers covered every label except the one the markup ships, which is the
tidy-looking version and does nothing: the first thing the script does is
overwrite the live label, and with `Theme` gone from the document the track
shrinks to `Dark` — the 12px shift, still there. Every candidate is a sizer
now, the live label included, and the children stretch instead of centring so
that nothing inside the button has a box that depends on the text in it.
Measured: the button is 61.516px and the nav's right edge 1170.484px for all
three labels, and a fresh load followed by four flips reports zero
layout-shift entries. It was 0.00007 with one shift on load.

And Lighthouse, re-run, moved its complaint rather than dropping it:
`--pending` at 2.46:1 on the "also known as" line and on the harvest
timestamp under the evidence strip. Same shape as `--overlay`, one rung
further down — the Face holds the quiet ladder to 1.5:1 because a checkpoint
nobody has reached is a row you skim past, and this site had it carrying the
denominator under a rate. `--skipped` is 4.44:1, which is a near miss and
therefore a miss. So the rule is the whole ladder, not one role: none of
`--overlay`, `--pending`, `--skipped` may be a text colour outside the 24px
cards, and tokens.test.mjs now says so for all three.

The page loses a layer for it — four greys for reading text become two — and
that is the right trade. A layer nobody can read is not a layer, and a second
muted token four hundredths of a ratio above the first would be a distinction
only a spreadsheet could see.
